{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:31:53.763Z","ordinal":186,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_4192d299be6065bbf62ca526","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["Hmm, `img-1180426539_vol-apple.ubifs` contains \"apple1\"?? That looks wrong — it should be a UBIFS image. Wait, maybe upstream changed the fixtures. Let me check: the output dir `fruits.ubi_extract` should contain `img-..._vol-apple.ubifs` and `img-..._vol-data.ubifs` (carved UBIFS volume images). But the downloaded file contains \"apple1\"... That's suspicious. Maybe upstream repo's current main differs from this checkout (61663627). The local checkout may have different expected outputs. I should fetch LFS objects matching the *local* pointer files' OIDs, not main branch.\n\nBetter approach: read each local pointer file, extract oid sha256, and download from the LFS batch API: `https://github.com/onekey-sec/unblob.git/info/lfs/objects/<oid>` — actually the LFS object URL format for GitHub: `https://media.githubusercontent.com/media/onekey-sec/unblob/<ref>/<path>` serves whatever is at that ref. For a specific oid, use the LFS API: POST to https://github.com/onekey-sec/unblob.git/info/lfs/objects/batch. Or simpler: `https://github.com/onekey-sec/unblob/raw/<commit>/<path>` redirects to media with the right oid for that commit.\n\nLocal checkout commit is 61663627. Let me use that ref. But wait — is local checkout == upstream main at some commit? git log shows merge commits from onekey-sec. Use the local HEAD sha as ref."]},"started_at_ms":1794659507219,"completed_at_ms":1794659513763}}
