{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:33:46.934Z","ordinal":204,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_7451cbf282e497cead9b54be","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["The oid matches — size 7, \"apple1\\n\". So upstream's expected output for `fruits.ubi_extract/img-..._vol-apple.ubifs` is literally the 7-byte text \"apple1\\n\"?! That means... the current UBI extraction flow is broken upstream: instead of carving the UBIFS volume image, the output is the extracted *content*? No wait — maybe the test expectation is that `ubireader_extract_images` produced volume files, and then... hmm, \"apple1\\n\" is the content of a file inside the apple volume.\n\nHold on — maybe the fruits.ubi volumes are tiny: the \"apple\" volume's UBIFS contains files apple1, apple2... and the expected output shows the volume image got extracted... but the file is named `img-1180426539_vol-apple.ubifs` with content \"apple1\\n\". That's weird. Unless the fixture itself was created oddly — e.g. the \"volumes\" in fruits.ubi are not UBIFS but raw data volumes containing the text \"apple1\\n\". That makes sense! fruits.ubi is a *synthetic* UBI image (4096 bytes) with two volumes \"apple\" and \"data\" whose LEBs just contain raw text, not UBIFS. So extraction yields the raw volume contents. And `orange.ubi.truncated.img` has real UBIFS volumes (rootfs, configuration) with orange1.txt etc.\n\nOK so the downloads are correct. Now copy all downloaded files over the local pointers and run the baseline tests."]},"started_at_ms":1794659622661,"completed_at_ms":1794659626934}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:33:46.939Z","ordinal":205,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"reasoning","id":"rs_7451cbf282e497cead9b54be","summary":[],"content":[{"type":"reasoning_text","text":"The oid matches — size 7, \"apple1\\n\". So upstream's expected output for `fruits.ubi_extract/img-..._vol-apple.ubifs` is literally the 7-byte text \"apple1\\n\"?! That means... the current UBI extraction flow is broken upstream: instead of carving the UBIFS volume image, the output is the extracted *content*? No wait — maybe the test expectation is that `ubireader_extract_images` produced volume files, and then... hmm, \"apple1\\n\" is the content of a file inside the apple volume.\n\nHold on — maybe the fruits.ubi volumes are tiny: the \"apple\" volume's UBIFS contains files apple1, apple2... and the expected output shows the volume image got extracted... but the file is named `img-1180426539_vol-apple.ubifs` with content \"apple1\\n\". That's weird. Unless the fixture itself was created oddly — e.g. the \"volumes\" in fruits.ubi are not UBIFS but raw data volumes containing the text \"apple1\\n\". That makes sense! fruits.ubi is a *synthetic* UBI image (4096 bytes) with two volumes \"apple\" and \"data\" whose LEBs just contain raw text, not UBIFS. So extraction yields the raw volume contents. And `orange.ubi.truncated.img` has real UBIFS volumes (rootfs, configuration) with orange1.txt etc.\n\nOK so the downloads are correct. Now copy all downloaded files over the local pointers and run the baseline tests."}],"encrypted_content":null,"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc"}}}
