{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:38:00.781Z","ordinal":238,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_04aa6765ee8d1ae814a9393d","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["Good. UBI EC header layout (from kernel ubi-media.h):\n\n```c\nstruct ubi_ec_hdr {\n\t__be32  magic;          // UBI_EC_HDR_MAGIC \"UBI#\"\n\t__u8    version;\n\t__u8    padding1[3];\n\t__be64  ec;\n\t__be32  vid_hdr_offset;\n\t__be32  data_offset;\n\t__be32  image_seq;\n\t__u8    padding2[32];\n\t__be32  hdr_crc;\n} __packed;  // 64 bytes\n```\n\nAll big-endian. hdr_crc = CRC32 of first 60 bytes.\n\nNow let me design the improved UBIHandler:\n\n```python\nclass UBIHandler(StructHandler):  # can now be StructHandler with C_DEFINITIONS for ec_hdr\n    NAME = \"ubi\"\n    PATTERNS = [HexString(\"55 42 49 23 01  // UBI# and version 1\")]\n    \n    C_DEFINITIONS = r\"\"\"\n        typedef struct ubi_ec_header {\n            uint32 magic;\n            uint8 version;\n            uint8 padding1[3];\n            uint64 ec;\n            uint32 vid_hdr_offset;\n            uint32 data_offset;\n            uint32 image_seq;\n            uint8 padding2[32];\n            uint32 hdr_crc;\n        } ubi_ec_header_t;\n    \"\"\"\n    HEADER_STRUCT = \"ubi_ec_header_t\"\n```\n\nEC header is big-endian always. StructHandler.parse_header(file, Endian.BIG).\n\nBad-block tolerance design:\n\n```python\n_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS = ... \n```\n\nHmm, what's a reasonable limit? Linux UBI default BEB limit is 20 (CONFIG_MTD_UBI_BEB_LIMIT). But for walking, a smaller limit reduces false continuation. With CRC validation, false continuation requires valid \"UBI#\" + version + valid CRC — extremely unlikely in random data. So we can afford a generous limit, say 20, matching the kernel's default bad eraseblock limit. Hmm, but wait: also erase counters... no.\n\nActually, do I want CRC validation in the walk? Consider: dumped images from real devices — EC headers of good PEBs have valid CRC. Bad PEBs: garbage/0xFF → invalid. Using CRC to distinguish is exactly what UBI does (\"bad PEBs are detected by corrupted EC headers\"). I'll validate magic+version+CRC in the walk. But keep not rejecting the chunk as a whole — the first header at start_offset: pattern already matched magic+version. If its CRC is invalid... could be a corrupt dump; we still chunk it (policy: extract anyway). For subsequent PEBs, CRC failure → bad block candidate.\n\nWait, subtlety: what about NAND images with ECC/OOB data interleaved? Out of scope — ubireader wouldn't handle those either.\n\nPEB size guessing — local GCD approach:\n\n```python\ndef _guess_peb_size(self, file, start_offset, ec_header_offsets):\n    # intervals between consecutive EC headers after start_offset\n    ...\n```\n\nBut iterate_patterns scans whole file — that's what current code does (scan whole file for \"UBI#\"). Keep that, then:\n\n```python\noffsets = [o for o in all_offsets if o >= start_offset]  # includes start_offset presumably\nintervals = get_intervals(offsets)  # between consecutive\npeb_size = gcd of first N intervals? or gcd of all local intervals until...?\n```\n\nProblem: if the blob contains a second UBI image later with a different PEB size, including its intervals in the gcd could shrink the gcd wrongly (gcd(p1*k, p2*m) could be gcd(p1,p2) < p1). E.g. p1=126976 (124KiB), p2=131072 (128KiB): gcd = 4096? 126976 = 31*4096, 131072 = 32*4096 → gcd 4096. Bad! So limit to intervals *within* this image: take intervals until... we don't know where the image ends — that's what we're computing. Chicken-and-egg.\n\nAlternative: use only the first few intervals (e.g. up to 4) after start_offset. If the image has ≥2 PEBs, first interval alone gives k*p where k = consecutive bad blocks + 1. gcd of first ~4 intervals is robust against bad blocks (need all 4 intervals to share factor >1 to fail — possible but unlikely) and against multi-image (only fails if image has <4 intervals, i.e. tiny images < 5 PEBs — plausible for small volumes! e.g. a 3-PEB config volume image followed by another image).\n\nHmm. Trade-offs. Maybe combine: compute gcd of ALL intervals from headers in the file that are... no.\n\nAlternative approach: iterative walk with unknown p: we know next EC header is at start + i1 where i1 = k1*p. We can compute candidate p = gcd of first few local intervals, then *verify* by walking: if walking with p immediately hits long runs of invalid PEBs and ends quickly while more \"UBI#\" headers exist beyond, p is wrong. Too complex. \n\nLet me keep it pragmatic: gcd of the first N=8 intervals following start_offset (or fewer if not available). Document the limitation in the doc. This handles bad blocks (gcd removes k factors) and localizes multi-image (only intervals within/near this image; if this image has ≥8 PEBs, all 8 intervals are local). For tiny images followed by different-sized images, gcd could still be off — acceptable, documented.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's an even better local signal: EC header contains `vid_hdr_offset` and `data_offset` — those are offsets *within* a PEB, not PEB size. No PEB size anywhere. OK.\n\nAlso `statistics.mode` import removed; use math.gcd.\n\nEdge: single PEB image (no intervals) → InvalidInputFormat as before? Current code raises InvalidInputFormat when no intervals. Keep.\n\nNow the walk with bad-block skipping:\n\n```python\ndef _walk_ubi(self, file, peb_size):\n    \"\"\"Walk PEBs from current offset; skip bad PEBs (invalid EC headers).\n\n    Returns end offset = end of the last valid PEB seen (including skipped bad PEBs before it).\n    \"\"\"\n    consecutive_bad = 0\n    end_offset = None  # offset after last valid PEB\n    while True:\n        offset = file.tell()\n        try:\n            ec_header = self.parse_header(file, Endian.BIG)\n        except EOFError:\n            break\n        if self._is_valid_ec_header(ec_header):\n            consecutive_bad = 0\n            end_offset = offset + peb_size\n        else:\n            consecutive_bad += 1\n            if consecutive_bad > MAX: break\n        try:\n            file.seek(offset + peb_size)\n        except SeekError:\n            break\n    if end_offset is None: raise InvalidInputFormat  # can't happen: first header matched pattern... but CRC might fail!\n```\n\nWait — first PEB: pattern matched magic+version but CRC not checked. If first PEB's CRC is invalid, with my scheme it's \"bad\" → counted as bad, walk continues... and end_offset stays None until a valid PEB. If none found within limit → InvalidInputFormat → chunk rejected. Hmm, is that desired? A corrupt image where the first EC header CRC fails but data is fine — we'd reject. Policy choice. To be lenient: treat the first PEB (at start_offset) as always valid (the pattern matched it). I'll special-case: first iteration counts as valid regardless of CRC. Actually simpler: initialize `end_offset = start_offset + peb_size`? No wait — cleaner: check validity as magic+version only for... hmm.\n\nLet me think about what \"valid\" means per purpose:\n- Purpose: distinguish \"this PEB belongs to the UBI image\" from \"image ended / bad block\".\n- For bad-block detection, UBI itself uses EC header CRC.\n- For the first PEB, pattern matched → definitely part of image → valid, skip CRC.\n\nImplementation: \n\n```python\ndef _is_valid_peb(self, ec_header) -> bool:\n    return (ec_header.magic == UBI_EC_MAGIC and ec_header.version == UBI_VERSION\n            and crc ok)\n```\n\nand in walk, first PEB: we know magic+version matched; still parse it; if CRC fails, log debug and treat as valid (corrupt dump tolerance)? Or treat as bad? I'll treat the first PEB as valid unconditionally (pattern gate already passed) — simplest, and consistent with \"extract corrupted anyway\" leaning.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's another consideration: should CRC mismatch on non-first PEBs mark them bad? What about a legitimately corrupt dump where many good PEBs have bad CRCs (e.g. dumped without... no, EC header CRC covers only the 64-byte header; if the dump is corrupt there, data is corrupt too). Real bad blocks → garbage EC → CRC fail. Good PEBs in a sane dump → CRC pass. I'm comfortable.\n\nBut hold on — what about images where EC headers were stripped or the dump is \"PEB data only\"? Not a thing for UBI images. OK.\n\nNow, what about `vid_hdr_offset`/`data_offset` validation? Could add sanity: vid_hdr_offset < data_offset <= peb_size, both aligned. These add gates against false \"UBI#\" in random data with accidental CRC... CRC already gates. Keep minimal: magic, version, CRC.\n\nMAX consecutive bad PEBs: kernel default BEB limit is 20 per 1024 PEBs. I'll use a constant `_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS = 20`? Hmm, if image genuinely ends and following data contains a valid-looking UBI# (another UBI image!) within 20 PEBs — that's actually the multi-image case: two UBI images concatenated. With bad-block skipping, image1's walk would absorb image2's PEBs as its own (they're valid EC headers!). Previously the walk would stop only at non-UBI# data. Wait — even the OLD code would absorb image2 if it's directly concatenated (all PEBs have UBI#). So concatenated images were always merged into one chunk. That's pre-existing behavior; ubireader handles multiple image_seq? Actually ubireader groups PEBs by image_seq into separate images! `ubireader_extract_images` creates one dir per image_seq? Let me check... ubireader's `get_images` groups blocks by image_seq. Yes, ubireader splits by image sequence. So merging concatenated images into one chunk is OK-ish for extraction.\n\nBut with different PEB sizes, the walk breaks (seeking by wrong peb_size lands mid-PEB, no valid EC header, ends after 20 bad). Fine — chunk covers image1 fully at least... wait no: if peb sizes differ, after image1 ends, seeking by p1 lands at non-PEB-boundary → invalid → 20 consecutive bad → end. end_offset = end of last valid PEB = end of image1 (if image1's end coincides... yes since image2 starts right after, and image1's last PEB is valid). \n\nBut the PEB size guess with local gcd: intervals after start_offset include the gap between image1's last header and image2's first header = p1*k + something? If p1 != p2, that interval isn't a multiple of p1 → gcd could become gcd(p1, weird) = small. With N=8 intervals and image1 having ≥8 PEBs, we never reach image2's intervals. Small images (<8 PEBs) are rare; document limitation. OK.\n\nAlternatively reduce N to 4? Bad-block robustness wants more intervals; multi-image wants fewer. I'll take intervals only up to... hmm, actually smarter: compute gcd incrementally and stop early if gcd becomes \"too small\" (< some min PEB size like 16 KiB? PEB sizes are typically ≥ 16KiB... actually can be 8KiB or even smaller). Meh. Keep it simple: use up to 8 intervals, gcd them. Also sanity-check peb_size: power of two? Real PEB sizes are powers of 2 (eraseblock sizes). If gcd result isn't power of two... e.g. intervals [2p, 4p] → gcd 2p (not power of two if p is). Hmm, can't distinguish. Skip that check? If gcd is not a power of two, maybe halve? Too clever. Keep gcd as-is, but validate peb_size > 0.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe simpler and closer to original: keep statistics.mode but on LOCAL intervals (from start_offset onward, first 8)? mode([p, 2p, p, p]) = p. With heavy bad blocks mode([2p,2p,4p]) = 2p → wrong. gcd([2p,2p,4p]) = 2p → also wrong. Both fail there. gcd handles [2p,3p,2p] → p; mode fails. gcd is strictly better here. Go with gcd.\n\nNow UBIFS handler validation. Superblock fields (from kernel ubifs-media.h):\n- ch.magic = UBIFS_NODE_MAGIC 0x06101831 (pattern matched)\n- ch.node_type = UBIFS_SB_NODE (6) (pattern matched)\n- key_hash: 0=R5, 1=test → must be <= 1? Vendors... it's kernel-defined enum, 2 values. Validate <= 1? Hmm \"Keep file values as plain int where vendors may add values\" — key_hash is kernel-defined, safe to gate? I'd gate lightly: fmt_version must be 1 (UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION... let me verify). Let me check ubireader's ubifs defines for SB node fields and values.\n\nLet me check what ubireader expects, to align validation with what the extractor accepts."]},"started_at_ms":1794659782757,"completed_at_ms":1794659880781}}
