{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:41:32.079Z","ordinal":267,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_b6c00347d282d00549ea4f05","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["ubireader: `~crc32(buf) & 0xFFFFFFFF`. zlib.crc32 already returns the final-xored CRC (zlib crc32 includes init 0 and final xor... actually zlib.crc32(data) == crc32 with init 0 internally xor'ed). Let me verify: `~zlib.crc32(ec[:60]) & 0xffffffff` should equal 0x3763f3bc. zlib.crc32(ec[:60]) = 0xc89c0c43 → ~ = 0x3763f3bc. Yes! So hdr_crc = ~zlib.crc32(hdr[:60]) & 0xFFFFFFFF. (Equivalent to zlib.crc32(hdr[:60], 0xFFFFFFFF)? No — 0x33717ab4 ≠. So use the complement form.)\n\nfruits.ubi: 4 PEBs of 1024 bytes. PEB0 = layout volume (vtbl with apple, data), PEB1..3 = volume data PEBs.\n\nNow let me look at VID header struct and vtbl record struct from ubireader defines to build the generator:\n\nVID_HDR_FORMAT / VTBL_REC_FORMAT — let me print them. Then write everything.\n\nAlso check `iterate_patterns` signature and `get_endian`. And StructHandler basics — look at another StructHandler for parse_header usage, e.g. jffs2 or cramfs. I know the pattern: `self.parse_header(file, endian)` uses HEADER_STRUCT. For UBIHandler converting to StructHandler: C_DEFINITIONS with big-endian parsing — cstruct default endianness? parse_header(file, Endian.BIG). EC header fields are BE. Fine.\n\nNow write the plan and implement. Let me define the new ubi.py.\n\n```python\nimport io\nimport math\nimport shutil\nimport zlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom structlog import get_logger\n\nfrom unblob.extractors import Command\n\nfrom ...file_utils import Endian, InvalidInputFormat, SeekError, get_endian\nfrom ...iter_utils import get_intervals\nfrom ...models import (File, Handler, HandlerDoc, HandlerType, HexString, Reference, StructHandler, ValidChunk)\n```\n\nUBIFSHandler changes: add validation in calculate_chunk:\n\n```python\n_UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION = 1\n_UBIFS_KEY_HASH_R5 = 0\n_UBIFS_KEY_HASH_TEST = 1\n_UBIFS_SIMPLE_KEY_FMT = 0\n_UBIFS_HASHED_KEY_FMT = 1\n_UBIFS_MIN_JHEADS_CNT = 1\n_UBIFS_COMPR_ZSTD = 3\n\ndef calculate_chunk(...):\n    endian = get_endian(file, self._BIG_ENDIAN_MAGIC)\n    sb_header = self.parse_header(file, endian)\n    self._validate_sb_node(sb_header)  # raises InvalidInputFormat\n    ...\n```\n\nValidation:\n```python\nif sb_header.fmt_version != 1: raise\nif sb_header.key_hash not in (0,1): raise\nif sb_header.key_fmt not in (0,1): raise\nif sb_header.jhead_cnt < 1: raise\nif sb_header.default_compr > 3: raise\nif sb_header.min_io_size == 0 or not power of two: raise\nif sb_header.leb_size == 0 or sb_header.leb_size % sb_header.min_io_size: raise? \n```\n\nHmm leb_size % min_io_size: is LEB size always a multiple of min_io_size? LEB size = PEB size - 2*min_io_size (EC+VID headers occupy 2 min_io blocks... actually vid_hdr_offset and data_offset are aligned to min_io_size; leb_size = peb_size - data_offset... no, LEB size = PEB size - EC hdr - VID hdr area). In practice leb_size is a multiple of min_io_size? mkfs.ubifs: leb_size = peb_size - 2 * min_io_size (when min_io_size >= 512ish). If peb_size is a multiple of min_io_size (it is, typically both powers of two), then leb_size is too. I think it's safe but is it *spec*? Kernel ubifs_media.md: \"LEB size... multiple of min. I/O unit size\"? I'm fairly confident LEB size is a multiple of min_io_size in valid images. Hmm, to be safe I could skip that check. Gates should protect chunk bounds: leb_size * leb_cnt. I'll require:\n- min_io_size: power of two, >= 8 (UBIFS min io is 8? kernel UBIFS_MIN_IO_SZ = 8? hmm not sure). Just require power of two and nonzero? min_io_size=1 → leb_size mult of 1... fine.\n- leb_size >= min_io_size (a LEB must hold at least one min I/O unit... plus headers; leb_size > min_io_size realistically).\n- leb_cnt >= UBIFS_MIN_LEB_CNT? Kernel: UBIFS_MIN_LEB_CNT = 15? Hmm — minimum LEB count for UBIFS is 15? I don't remember exactly; there is UBIFS_MIN_LEB_CNT... I don't want to invent. Skip; require leb_cnt > 0.\n\nKeep gates: fmt_version==1, key_hash<=1, key_fmt<=1, jhead_cnt>=1, default_compr<=3, min_io_size power-of-two nonzero, leb_size nonzero multiple of min_io_size, leb_cnt nonzero. Hmm \"multiple of min_io_size\" — risk: some vendor image with odd leb_size? mkfs.ubifs computes leb_size = peb_size - 2*min_io_size; peb_size multiple of min_io_size (both powers of 2 normally, min_io_size ≤ peb_size) → leb_size multiple. I'll keep it; it directly guards the chunk-size computation. Actually wait, also ro_compat_version — UBIFS_RO_COMPAT_VERSION = 0? kernel: UBIFS_RO_COMPAT_VERSION = 0? There are ro-compat versions... skip.\n\nWhere to put validation: a module-level function or classmethod on UBIFSHandler. AGENTS.md: \"Functions used by one class belong in that class.\" → staticmethod/classmethod.\n\nUBIHandler rework:\n\n```python\nclass UBIHandler(StructHandler):\n    NAME = \"ubi\"\n\n    _UBI_EC_MAGIC = 0x55424923  # \"UBI#\"\n    _UBI_EC_VERSION = 1\n    # UBI EC/VID headers are big-endian\n    _ENDIAN = Endian.BIG\n\n    # Maximum number of consecutive bad PEBs (invalid EC headers) we skip over\n    # before deciding the UBI image ended. The Linux UBI bad eraseblock limit\n    # defaults to 20 (CONFIG_MTD_UBI_BEB_LIMIT).\n    _MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS = 20\n\n    # Number of erase-block intervals used to guess the PEB size...\n    _PEB_SIZE_GUESS_INTERVALS = 8\n\n    PATTERNS = [HexString(\"55 42 49 23 01  // UBI# and version 1\")]\n\n    C_DEFINITIONS = r\"\"\"\n        // UBI eraseblock counter header, see ubi-media.h in the Linux kernel\n        typedef struct ubi_ec_header {\n            uint32 magic;\n            uint8 version;\n            uint8 padding1[3];\n            uint64 ec;\n            uint32 vid_hdr_offset;\n            uint32 data_offset;\n            uint32 image_seq;\n            uint8 padding2[32];\n            uint32 hdr_crc;\n        } ubi_ec_header_t;\n    \"\"\"\n    HEADER_STRUCT = \"ubi_ec_header_t\"\n```\n\nWait — StructHandler parse_header uses HEADER_STRUCT and computes header size. For UBIHandler we parse at arbitrary offsets during the walk; parse_header(file, Endian.BIG) parses at current position. Good.\n\nEC header CRC check:\n\n```python\ndef _ec_header_crc_valid(self, file?) \n```\n\nProblem: parse_header parses fields but CRC is over raw bytes. Need raw 60 bytes. Approach: in walk, read 64 bytes, parse from bytes: `self._struct_parser.parse(header_bytes, Endian.BIG)`? StructHandler has `self._struct_parser`? Let me check StructHandler/StructParser API in models. Let me look at how other handlers parse from bytes. AGENTS.md: \"StructParser.parse takes a File or bytes\". I'll check models.py for exact API.\n\nCRC: `hdr_crc == (~zlib.crc32(header_bytes[:60]) & 0xFFFFFFFF)`? Wait — kernel computes crc32(UBI_CRC32_INIT, hdr, 60) where UBI_CRC32_INIT = 0xFFFFFFFF? ubireader checks `self.crc != (~crc32(buf_crc) & 0xFFFFFFFF)` where buf_crc is first 60 bytes... wait let me check ubireader headers.py line 62 context: `crc_chk = (~crc32(buf_crc) & UBI_CRC32_INIT)` — which crc32? `from zlib import crc32`. So valid iff hdr_crc == ~zlib.crc32(hdr[:60]) & 0xFFFFFFFF. Verified against fruits.ubi above (0xc89c0c43 complement = 0x3763f3bc ✓).\n\nIn Python, cleaner: `(zlib.crc32(header[:60]) ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) == hdr_crc`.\n\nWalk algorithm:\n\n```python\ndef _walk_ubi(self, file: File, peb_size: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Walk PEBs from the current offset, skipping bad eraseblocks.\n\n    Bad eraseblocks (PEBs without a valid EC header) are skipped, up to\n    _MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS in a row, after which we consider the image\n    finished. Returns the end offset of the last valid PEB.\n    \"\"\"\n    consecutive_bad_pebs = 0\n    end_offset = file.tell()  # will be advanced past the first (matched) PEB below\n    while True:\n        offset = file.tell()\n        ec_header = ... read 64 bytes ... \n        if eof/short: break\n        if self._is_valid_ec_header(...):\n            consecutive_bad_pebs = 0\n            end_offset = offset + peb_size\n        else:\n            consecutive_bad_pebs += 1\n            if consecutive_bad_pebs > self._MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS:\n                break\n        try:\n            file.seek(offset + peb_size)\n        except SeekError:\n            break\n    return end_offset\n```\n\nFirst PEB: pattern matched magic+version but not CRC. If first PEB CRC invalid → counted bad; if no valid PEB found at all → end_offset stays at start → chunk start==end → unblob rejects? \"chunks that end at or before their start\" are rejected by unblob — but calculate_chunk returning such chunk... AGENTS.md says don't re-check what unblob rejects. But nicer to raise InvalidInputFormat if no valid PEB found? Hmm: if first PEB's CRC is bad but image is otherwise fine (corrupt dump), we'd want to still extract. Treat the first PEB as valid: initialize `end_offset = start_offset + peb_size`? But _walk_ubi doesn't know start_offset... it's at file position. Let me pass start_offset or handle in calculate_chunk: the first PEB is known-good (pattern matched), so:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_chunk(self, file, start_offset):\n    peb_size = self._guess_peb_size(file, start_offset)\n    file.seek(start_offset)\n    end_offset = self._walk_ubi(file, peb_size)\n    return ValidChunk(...)\n```\n\nwith _walk_ubi treating the first PEB as valid regardless of CRC:\n\n```python\nfirst = True\n...\nif first or self._is_valid_ec_header(header_bytes):\n    ...\nfirst = False\n```\n\nHmm wait, but reading: need to read 64 bytes; at EOF read returns short → break. `file.read(64)`; check len == 64 else break (AGENTS.md: plain read returns short silently; check length).\n\nBut hold on — reading 64 bytes per PEB via parse from bytes: `self._struct_parser.parse(header_bytes, Endian.BIG)`. Let me check StructParser A