{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:45:06.874Z","ordinal":293,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_6b5609d83c7002ca2126d6c1","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["iterate_patterns restores file position when exhausted (\"seek back to the initial position\"). But if I break out of the generator early... I'll materialize with list() so it completes and restores position. Then I validate offsets by seeking — fine afterwards.\n\nStructParser.parse(struct_name, file_or_bytes, endian). Good.\n\nNow let me check StructHandler for how C_DEFINITIONS get parsed and whether there's a `__init__` — StructHandler.__init__ creates _struct_parser. UBIHandler currently extends Handler; converting to StructHandler is fine.\n\nLet me now write the plan and the new ubi.py.\n\nOne more design point for UBIFSHandler: the TODO comment about matching only superblocks — keep behavior, but the comment block can stay (maybe trimmed). Keep it.\n\nAlso `Handler` import may become unused. Check imports after edit.\n\nNew ubi.py:\n\n```python\nimport math\nimport shutil\nimport zlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom structlog import get_logger\n\nfrom unblob.extractors import Command\n\nfrom ...file_utils import (\n    Endian,\n    InvalidInputFormat,\n    SeekError,\n    get_endian,\n    iterate_patterns,\n)\nfrom ...iter_utils import get_intervals\nfrom ...models import (\n    File,\n    HandlerDoc,\n    HandlerType,\n    HexString,\n    Reference,\n    StructHandler,\n    ValidChunk,\n)\n\nlogger = get_logger()\n\n_UBI_EC_HEADER_MAGIC = b\"UBI#\"\n_UBI_EC_HEADER_SIZE = 64\n_UBI_EC_HEADER_CRC_OFFSET = 60\n_UBI_VERSION_1 = 1\n```\n\nUBIFSHandler: add validation. Where do constants live — class-level.\n\n```python\nclass UBIFSHandler(StructHandler):\n    NAME = \"ubifs\"\n    ...\n    # https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.x/fs/ubifs/ubifs-media.h\n    _UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION = 1\n    _UBIFS_KEY_HASH_R5 = 0\n    _UBIFS_KEY_HASH_TEST = 1\n    _UBIFS_SIMPLE_KEY_FMT = 0\n    _UBIFS_HASHED_KEY_FMT = 1\n    _UBIFS_MIN_JHEADS_CNT = 1\n    _UBIFS_COMPR_NONE = 0\n    _UBIFS_COMPR_ZSTD = 3\n```\n\nWait, AGENTS.md says use IntEnum for spec codes. These are only used for validation comparisons; IntEnum for compression type etc. Let me use IntEnums:\n\n```python\nclass ComprType(IntEnum): NONE=0, LZO=1, ZLIB=2, ZSTD=3\n```\n\nHmm, but \"Keep file values as plain int where vendors may add values\" — these are kernel-defined, not vendor. But do I need enums? Validation: `if not (ComprType.NONE <= sb.default_compr <= ComprType.ZSTD)`. Simpler with plain constants. The AGENTS.md guidance: \"Use IntEnum / IntFlag for spec codes.\" I'll add small IntEnums for key_hash/key_fmt/compr where used in checks. Actually to keep the diff focused, I'll validate with module constants — hmm, instructions say to use IntEnum for spec codes. Let me define:\n\n```python\nclass UBIFSKeyHash(IntEnum):\n    R5 = 0\n    TEST = 1\n\nclass UBIFSKeyFmt(IntEnum):\n    SIMPLE = 0\n    HASHED = 1\n\nclass UBIFSCompr(IntEnum):\n    NONE = 0\n    LZO = 1\n    ZLIB = 2\n    ZSTD = 3\n```\n\nand validate membership via try/except ValueError? `IntEnum(value)` raises on unknown — but these are spec-complete, so raising InvalidInputFormat on unknown is right:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    UBIFSKeyHash(sb.key_hash)\n    UBIFSKeyFmt(sb.key_fmt)\n    UBIFSCompr(sb.default_compr)\nexcept ValueError as exc:\n    raise InvalidInputFormat(...) from exc\n```\n\nHmm wait, but key_fmt HASHED... is UBIFS_HASHED_KEY_FMT = 1 real? Kernel ubifs-media.h: `#define UBIFS_SIMPLE_KEY_FMT 0` and `#define UBIFS_HASHED_KEY_FMT 1`. Yes. key_hash: UBIFS_KEY_HASH_R5 0, UBIFS_KEY_HASH_TEST 1. Yes.\n\nfmt_version: UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION 1. jhead_cnt: min 1 (UBIFS_MIN_JHEADS_CNT 1). default_compr 0-3. \n\nmin_io_size: must be power of 2 (kernel: min_io_size is the minimal I/O unit, power of 2). Check `min_io_size > 0 and min_io_size & (min_io_size-1) == 0`. AGENTS.md says avoid hand-written masks... \"Describe on-disk structures with StructHandler... read fields by name, not hand-written offsets, masks or slices\" — that's about parsing structures, not a power-of-two check. A power-of-two check via bit trick is fine, or use `math.log2`... bit trick is clearest.\n\nleb_size: multiple of min_io_size and >= some minimum? I'll check `leb_size >= min_io_size` and `leb_size % min_io_size == 0`. Hmm — is leb_size always a multiple of min_io_size? Let me double check with the banana fixtures: parse their superblocks and verify all my gates pass on real fixtures (banana.*.ubifs and the orange volumes). I'll write a quick verification script after implementing.\n\nleb_cnt: > 0. Also maybe max_leb_cnt >= leb_cnt? Could add: `leb_cnt <= max_leb_cnt`. That's a real invariant. Add it.\n\nAlso validate ch.len? The common header len for sb node = 4096 (UBIFS_SB_NODE_SZ). Could check `sb_header.ch.len == 4096`? That's a strong gate. Kernel UBIFS_SB_NODE_SZ = 4096. Real images always 4096. Add it? It's spec-defined constant. I'll add — strong false-positive gate. Hmm, but wait: does ubireader care? Doesn't matter; gate is for chunking. I'll include len check. Actually, let me be a bit careful: some very old UBIFS fmt? fmt_version 1 always, sb node 4096. Keep.\n\nNow the UBIHandler:\n\n```python\nclass UBIHandler(StructHandler):\n    NAME = \"ubi\"\n\n    PATTERNS = [HexString(\"55 42 49 23 01  // UBI# and version 1\")]\n\n    C_DEFINITIONS = r\"\"\"\n        // UBI eraseblock counter (EC) header.\n        // https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/include/uapi/mtd/ubi-user.h\n        // include/uapi/mtd/ubi-user.h? no — EC header is in drivers/mtd/ubi/ubi-media.h\n        typedef struct ubi_ec_header {\n            uint32 magic;\n            uint8 version;\n            uint8 padding1[3];\n            uint64 ec;\n            uint32 vid_hdr_offset;\n            uint32 data_offset;\n            uint32 image_seq;\n            uint8 padding2[32];\n            uint32 hdr_crc;\n        } ubi_ec_header_t;\n    \"\"\"\n    HEADER_STRUCT = \"ubi_ec_header_t\"\n```\n\nReference: ubi-media.h in kernel: https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.16/drivers/mtd/ubi/ubi-media.h — pin to a version tag. Let me use v6.16 (exists? current 2026 — v6.16 exists, sure; even v6.12). I'll cite `https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/drivers/mtd/ubi/ubi-media.h`.\n\nMethods:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def _ec_header_crc_valid(header_bytes: bytes, hdr_crc: int) -> bool:\n        crc = zlib.crc32(header_bytes[:_UBI_EC_HEADER_CRC_OFFSET]) ^ 0xFFFFFFFF\n        return crc == hdr_crc\n```\n\nHmm, cleaner: a helper `_parse_ec_header(file, offset) -> header | None`:\n\n```python\n    def _read_valid_ec_header(self, file: File, offset: int):\n        \"\"\"Read the EC header at offset; return it only if magic, version and CRC are valid.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            file.seek(offset)\n        except SeekError:\n            return None\n        header_bytes = file.read(_UBI_EC_HEADER_SIZE)\n        if len(header_bytes) != _UBI_EC_HEADER_SIZE:\n            return None\n        ec_header = self._struct_parser.parse(self.HEADER_STRUCT, header_bytes, Endian.BIG)\n        if ec_header.magic != _UBI_EC_MAGIC or ec_header.version != _UBI_VERSION_1:\n            return None\n        crc = zlib.crc32(header_bytes[:_UBI_EC_HEADER_CRC_OFFSET]) ^ 0xFFFFFFFF\n        if crc != ec_header.hdr_crc:\n            return None\n        return ec_header\n```\n\nWait — StructParser.parse signature: parse(struct_name, source, endian)? From StructHandler.parse_header: `self._struct_parser.parse(self.HEADER_STRUCT, file, endian)`. So yes.\n\nmagic as parsed uint32 BE: \"UBI#\" = 0x55424923.\n\n_guess_peb_size:\n\n```python\n    def _guess_peb_size(self, file: File, start_offset: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Guess the PEB size from the EC headers local to this UBI image.\n\n        Bad eraseblocks make intervals between valid EC headers multiples of\n        the PEB size, so the GCD of the first few intervals following\n        start_offset gives the PEB size. Looking at the whole file instead\n        would break on blobs containing multiple UBI images with different\n        PEB sizes.\n        \"\"\"\n        ec_header_offsets = [\n            offset\n            for offset in iterate_patterns(file, _UBI_EC_HEADER_MAGIC)\n            if offset >= start_offset\n            and self._read_valid_ec_header(file, offset) is not None\n        ]\n        intervals = get_intervals(ec_header_offsets)[: _PEB_SIZE_GUESS_INTERVALS]\n        if not intervals:\n            raise InvalidInputFormat(\n                \"UBI image has a single eraseblock, cannot guess the PEB size\"\n            )\n        return math.gcd(*intervals)\n```\n\nWait — iterate_patterns restores position only when exhausted; the list comprehension exhausts it. But I seek inside the loop (`_read_valid_ec_header` seeks) WHILE iterating — that breaks iterate_patterns (it uses file position for chunk scanning)! Must materialize offsets first:\n\n```python\n        candidate_offsets = list(iterate_patterns(file, _UBI_EC_HEADER_MAGIC))\n        ec_header_offsets = [\n            offset for offset in candidate_offsets\n            if offset >= start_offset and self._read_valid_ec_header(file, offset) is not None\n        ]\n```\n\nGood catch.\n\n_walk_ubi:\n\n```python\n    def _walk_ubi(self, file: File, peb_size: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Walk PEBs from the current offset, skipping bad eraseblocks.\n\n        PEBs without a valid EC header are bad eraseblocks: skip up to\n        _MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS of them in a row before deciding the image\n        ended. Returns the end offset of the last valid PEB, so that skipped\n        bad eraseblocks stay inside the chunk and PEB numbering is preserved\n        for the extractor.\n        \"\"\"\n        consecutive_bad_pebs = 0\n        end_offset = offset = file.tell()\n        first_peb = True\n        while True:\n            offset = file.tell()\n            # The first PEB matched our pattern, so we know it belongs to the\n            # image even if its header CRC does not check out.\n            if first_peb or self._read_valid_ec_header(file, offset) is not None:\n                consecutive_bad_pebs = 0\n                end_offset = offset + peb_size\n            else:\n                consecutive_bad_pebs += 1\n                if consecutive_bad_pebs > self._MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS:\n                    break\n            first_peb = False\n            try:\n                file.seek(offset + peb_size)\n            except SeekError:\n                break\n        return end_offset\n```\n\nHmm wait, `_read_valid_ec_header` seeks to offset — but we're already at offset; it seeks to same place, fine. But after `_read_valid_ec_header`, file position is offset+64; then `file.seek(offset + peb_size)` — absolute seek, fine.\n\nEdge: `file.seek(offset + peb_size)` beyond EOF — does File.seek raise SeekError beyond end? Original code caught SeekError on seek, so yes it can raise. But seeking exactly to EOF is allowed presumably; then next loop read returns short → `_read_valid_ec_header` returns None → counts as bad... then we'd loop 20 more times seeking further and further past EOF — seek past EOF raises SeekError? If seek beyond EOF raises, we break immediately. If seek to EOF+peb is allowed (sparse), we'd count bads until limit. Original code: read 4 bytes, b\"\" → break. My version: read 64 → short → None → bad+1, seek further... if seeks keep succeeding (file objects usually allow seeking past end!), we'd do 21 iterations then break. Slightly wasteful but bounded. Hmm, but wait: can File.seek past EOF succeed? Python file objects allow it. Original code relied on read()==b\"\" to break, not SeekError. So for my loop, past-EOF reads give short reads → bad count increments → up to 21 extra iterations of harmless reads. Acceptable but ugly; better: track EOF? AGENTS.md says don't check file.size()... \"Don't clip to the file or check file.size()\" — that's about chunk sizes. For walk termination, reading and getting short data is the natural EOF signal. I could break immediately when the read returns empty (b\"\") vs short-but-nonempty (truncated header at EOF → also end). Let me restructure: _read_valid_ec_header returns None for both invalid and unreadable; but for walk termination I want to distinguish \"no more data\" from \"bad block\". Simplest: in _walk_ubi, do the read itself:\n\nActually cleaner: make _read_valid_ec_header return None only for invalid content, and raise/propagate a signal for EOF? Hmm. Alternative: in walk, peek: \n\n```python\n        while True:\n            offset = file.tell()\n            header_bytes = file.read(_UBI_EC_HEADER_SIZE)\n            if len(header_bytes) < _UBI_EC_HEADER_MAGIC_LEN? \n```\n\nLet me just do: if `len(header_bytes) == 0` → break (EOF). If `0 < len < 64` → truncated tail → break (can't be a valid PEB; treat as end of image). Else parse & validate.\n\nSo _walk_ubi does its own reading, and _read_valid_ec_header(file, offset) is used only by _guess_peb_size. To avoid duplication, factor a `_parse_ec_header(header_bytes) -> ec_header | None` (pure, from bytes) used by both:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_ec_header(self, header_bytes: bytes):\n        if len(header_bytes) != _UBI_EC_HEADER_SIZE: return None\n        ec_header = self._struct_parser.parse(self.HEADER_STRUCT, header_bytes, Endian.BIG)\n        if magic/version/crc invalid: return None\n        return ec_header\n```\n\n_walk_ubi:\n\n```python\n        while True:\n            offset = file.tell()\n            header_bytes = file.read(_UBI_EC_HEADER_SIZE)\n            if len(header_bytes) != _UBI_EC_HEADER_SIZE:\n                break  # ran past the end of the file\n            if first_peb or self._parse_ec_header(header_bytes) is not None:\n                ...\n```\n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: reading 64 bytes at each PEB — if a \"bad block\" region is actually shorter... no, PEB grid is fixed; bad PEBs occupy full PEB size. OK.\n\nBut hold on, one more scenario: what if peb_size guess is wrong (too small, e.g. gcd collapsed)? Then reads land mid-PEB, garbage → 21 bads → end. Chunk = first PEB only... could even produce tiny chunks. Same failure mode as before (mode-guess wrong). Fine.\n\ncalculate_chunk:\n\n```python\n    def calculate_chunk(self, file: File, start_offset: int) -> ValidChunk | None:\n        peb_size = self._guess_peb_size(file, start_offset)\n        logger.debug(\"Guessed UBI PEB size\", size=peb_size)\n        file.seek(start_offset)\n        end_offset = self._walk_ubi(file, peb_size)\n        return ValidChunk(start_offset=start_offset, end_offset=end_offset)\n```\n\nReturn type: existing code returns ValidChunk (not Optional). Keep signature as `-> ValidChunk | None`? Original had `-> ValidChunk | None`. Keep.\n\nConstants:\n\n```python\n_UBI_EC_HEADER_MAGIC = b\"UBI#\"\n_UBI_EC_HEADER_MAGIC_UINT32 = 0x55424923\n_UBI_EC_HEADER_SIZE = 64\n_UBI_EC_HEADER_CRC_DATA_SIZE = 60\n_UBI_VERSION_1 = 1\n# Linux UBI defaults to reserving at most 20 PEBs per 1024 for bad eraseblock\n# replacement (CONFIG_MTD_UBI_BEB_LIMIT)...\n_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS = 20\n_PEB_SIZE_GUESS_INTERVALS = 8\n```\n\nWhere to put constants — module level or class? AGENTS.md: functions used by one class belong in that class. Constants similar → class attributes. I'll make them class-level on UBIHandler (except maybe magic bytes used in PATTERNS... PATTERNS uses HexString literal anyway).\n\nHandlerDoc updates:\n\nUBI:\n- description: mention multi-volume splitting, bad eraseblock skipping, per-volume UBIFS extraction via ubireader. \n- limitations: PEB size is guessed from EC header intervals (GCD of first 8 intervals); images with a single PEB are not supported; concatenated images with different PEB sizes where the first image has fewer than ... hmm keep concise: \"The PEB size is not stored in UBI headers and is guessed from eraseblock header intervals, which can fail for very small images.\" \n\nUBIFS:\n- description: mention superblock validation.\n- limitations: keep existing note? Current limitations=[] and there's a TODO comment about only matching superblock-first images. Add limitation: \"Only images starting with a superblock node are recognized.\" That's a real limitation. \n\nNow the tests. tests/handlers/filesystem/test_ubi.py:\n\nHelper to build synthetic UBI images:\n\n```python\nPEB_SIZE = 1024\nEC_HEADER_SIZE = 64\n\ndef ec_header(ec=0, vid_hdr_offset=64, data_offset=128, image_seq=1, corrupt_crc=False):\n    hdr = struct.pack(\">4sB3sQIII32s\", b\"UBI#\", 1, b\"\\0\"*3, ec, vid_hdr_offset, data_offset, image_seq, b\"\\0\"*32)\n    crc = zlib.crc32(hdr) ^ 0xFFFFFFFF\n    if corrupt_crc: crc ^= 0xFF\n    return hdr + struct.pack(\">I\", crc)\n\ndef peb(valid=True): -> bytes: ec_header + data fill\ndef build_ubi_image(peb_count, bad_pebs=(), peb_size=PEB_SIZE): ...\n```\n\nTests:\n1. `test_calculate_chunk_simple`: image of 4 valid PEBs → chunk (0, 4*1024). Also embedded at nonzero offset: prefix garbage + image → calculate_chunk(handler, content, start_offset=len(prefix)) → end correct. Use testing.calculate_chunk.\n2. `test_calculate_chunk_with_bad_blocks`: 8 PEBs, bad at 3 and 6 → chunk covers all 8 PEBs (0, 8192).\n3. `test_calculate_chunk_stops_after_too_many_bad_blocks`: 3 valid + 21+ bad (garbage without EC magic) + ... → chunk ends after last valid PEB (3*1024). Also trailing garbage: image followed by non-UBI data → end at image end.\n4. `test_calculate_chunk_multiple_images_different_peb_sizes`: image A (peb 1024, 6 PEBs) + image B (peb 2048, 4 PEBs) → chunk for A ends at 6*1024. And calculate_chunk at B's start → (6144, 6144+8192). Wait — walk for A: after A's PEBs, seeking by 1024 lands at 6144 (B's start, valid EC header with dif