d parsed.image_seq == image_seq: valid PEB\nelif parsed is not None: # valid EC header of ANOTHER image -> image ended\n    break\nelse: bad block\n```\n\nAnd _guess_peb_size should also only consider headers with matching image_seq! Otherwise intervals into the next image pollute the gcd. With seq filtering: A's headers at 0..5120 step 1024 → intervals all 1024 → gcd 1024 ✓. B's: 2048 ✓. \n\nBut wait — what if image_seq varies within one image due to corruption? EC CRC valid but seq different — that'd be a crafted/corrupt case; treating as image boundary is defensible.\n\nHmm, but one more: the first PEB's image_seq — first PEB is pattern-matched but CRC might be invalid (corrupt). Then we can't trust its seq. Use the seq of the first *valid* EC header ≥ start_offset as the image's seq. In _guess_peb_size I already filter valid headers ≥ start_offset; take seq from the first of those, filter rest by same seq. Then walk uses that seq. Pass seq from calculate_chunk:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_chunk(...):\n    peb_size, image_seq = self._guess_peb_size(file, start_offset)  # hmm\n```\n\nLet me restructure: \n\n```python\ndef calculate_chunk(self, file, start_offset):\n    image_seq = self._get_image_seq(file, start_offset)  # first valid EC header at/after start_offset; None if first PEB itself invalid-CRC and no other? \n    peb_size = self._guess_peb_size(file, start_offset, image_seq)\n    ...\n```\n\nSimplify: _guess_peb_size computes valid offsets (filtered by seq of first valid header). It can return (peb_size, image_seq). Then _walk_ubi(file, peb_size, image_seq).\n\nEdge: ALL headers invalid CRC (corrupt dump) → no valid offsets → no intervals → InvalidInputFormat. Old code would've chunked it. Regression for corrupt dumps! Hmm. Old behavior: any \"UBI#\" occurrences (no CRC) → mode of intervals → walk on magic only. New: requires ≥2 valid EC headers. A dump with all CRCs broken is pathological; but a dump with *some* bad blocks is exactly our target case and