{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:45:51.395Z","ordinal":302,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_b5f0ee1d44c1a9e0806b7f0b","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["ubireader records CRC errors but doesn't reject blocks on EC CRC failure (just logs/adds to errors). So ubireader extracts even with bad EC CRCs. That means if I gate chunking on EC CRC validity, a corrupt-but-extractable image could be rejected by unblob. Hmm.\n\nTrade-off:\n- CRC gating in walk: robust bad-block detection (distinguishes bad PEB from random garbage with \"UBI#\" — actually garbage with \"UBI#\" AND version 1 AND valid CRC is near-impossible randomly; garbage with just \"UBI#\"+version is plausible in random data? 5 bytes → 2^40, not plausible either).\n\nHonestly, magic+version (5 bytes) alone is already a strong per-PEB signal. The old walk used magic only (4 bytes). The bad-block problem: bad PEB has garbage → no magic → old walk stops. New walk: skip up to 20. Do I even need CRC for the walk? The risk without CRC: random data after image end containing \"UBI#\\x01\" within 20 PEBs → chunk over-extends. 5-byte signature in random data at a specific offset: 2^-40 per PEB; over 20 PEBs still negligible. But in *structured* firmware data (not random), \"UBI#\" could appear... e.g., another UBI image follows (multi-image!) — handled by image_seq check, not CRC. A bad PEB whose garbage happens to contain \"UBI#\\x01\" at offset 0 → counted as valid → walk continues with wrong alignment? No — alignment maintained by peb_size steps; a false positive just resets the bad counter. Bounded damage.\n\nWhere CRC really helps: _guess_peb_size filtering false \"UBI#\" occurrences inside PEB *data* (not at PEB boundaries). E.g., UBIFS data containing \"UBI#\" bytes → false offset → interval not multiple of peb → gcd collapses. With magic-only filtering, a single false occurrence breaks the gcd. With CRC filtering, false occurrences are dropped. That's valuable. But the corrupt-dump regression: all-CRC-broken image → InvalidInputFormat → no chunk (old code worked). \n\nMiddle ground: filter candidate offsets by CRC *if enough valid headers exist*; else fall back to magic+version only:\n\n```python\nvalid = [o for o in candidates if o >= start and self._parse_ec_header(...) is not None]\nif len(valid) >= 2: use valid\nelse: use magic+version-filtered offsets (o >= start)\n```\n\nHmm, adds complexity. Is it justified? The corrupt-EC-everywhere dump is pathological; but \"never silently fail to extract what we used to extract\" matters. The fallback is ~4 lines. I'll do: try CRC-validated offsets first; if fewer than 2, fall back to version-checked offsets (magic+version, no CRC). Actually wait — for the walk, similar concern: if I require CRC validity to count a PEB as good, an all-corrupt-CRC image walks only PEB0 (first_peb special-cased) then 20 bads → chunk = 1 PEB. Old code would walk the whole image. Regression! So the walk should also adapt: use CRC-validity if the image's headers are generally CRC-valid, else magic+version.\n\nSimpler unified approach: decide per-image \"strictness\" in calculate_chunk: \n\n```python\nimage_offsets_valid = crc-valid offsets >= start\nif len(valid) >= 2: strict (use CRC+seq) else: lenient (magic+version)\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me reconsider — how likely is a firmware dump with systematically broken EC CRCs? EC header CRC is written by ubinize/UBI itself; dumps preserve it. Broken-CRC-everywhere basically doesn't happen in practice (unlike JFFS2 where CRC quirks are common). ubireader computes and reports it. Real bad blocks → garbage. I'll go strict (CRC required for non-first PEBs), no fallback. It gives clean semantics:\n- valid EC header (magic+version+CRC) + matching image_seq → same image, continue\n- valid EC header + different image_seq → another image → stop\n- invalid → bad block → skip up to 20\n\nAnd document in limitations: \"PEBs are validated with their EC header CRC; images with systematically corrupted eraseblock headers are not fully recognized.\" Hmm wait, actually, let me reconsider once more... The user's scenario is *automotive firmware with bad blocks* — standard ubinize images + bad blocks. Strict CRC works perfectly there. I'll go strict. And the first PEB is exempt (pattern-matched; also handles the case where PEB0's CRC is broken but rest are fine).\n\nWait, one more edge in _guess_peb_size with seq: I filter offsets by (valid CRC) and (seq == seq of first valid offset ≥ start_offset). But what if start_offset's PEB has broken CRC and the *next* valid header belongs to a *different* concatenated image? Pathological; ignore.\n\nNow — hmm, actually one more important case: what if the same blob contains the same UBI image's PEBs but the blob also has OTHER \"UBI#\" data far away (e.g. another partition with a UBI image of same seq? unlikely). Fine.\n\nLet me also double check `get_endian` usage stays for UBIFS. Yes.\n\nNow write everything. Also check `Handler` import still needed (UBIHandler becomes StructHandler; UBIFSHandler already StructHandler). `iterate_patterns` still used. `statistics` import dropped, add `math`, `zlib`. `SeekError` still used in walk. `get_intervals` still used.\n\nLet me now check models.StructHandler more closely and Endian import location."]},"started_at_ms":1794660317418,"completed_at_ms":1794660351395}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:45:51.402Z","ordinal":303,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"reasoning","id":"rs_b5f0ee1d44c1a9e0806b7f0b","summary":[],"content":[{"type":"reasoning_text","text":"ubireader records CRC errors but doesn't reject blocks on EC CRC failure (just logs/adds to errors). So ubireader extracts even with bad EC CRCs. That means if I gate chunking on EC CRC validity, a corrupt-but-extractable image could be rejected by unblob. Hmm.\n\nTrade-off:\n- CRC gating in walk: robust bad-block detection (distinguishes bad PEB from random garbage with \"UBI#\" — actually garbage with \"UBI#\" AND version 1 AND valid CRC is near-impossible randomly; garbage with just \"UBI#\"+version is plausible in random data? 5 bytes → 2^40, not plausible either).\n\nHonestly, magic+version (5 bytes) alone is already a strong per-PEB signal. The old walk used magic only (4 bytes). The bad-block problem: bad PEB has garbage → no magic → old walk stops. New walk: skip up to 20. Do I even need CRC for the walk? The risk without CRC: random data after image end containing \"UBI#\\x01\" within 20 PEBs → chunk over-extends. 5-byte signature in random data at a specific offset: 2^-40 per PEB; over 20 PEBs still negligible. But in *structured* firmware data (not random), \"UBI#\" could appear... e.g., another UBI image follows (multi-image!) — handled by image_seq check, not CRC. A bad PEB whose garbage happens to contain \"UBI#\\x01\" at offset 0 → counted as valid → walk continues with wrong alignment? No — alignment maintained by peb_size steps; a false positive just resets the bad counter. Bounded damage.\n\nWhere CRC really helps: _guess_peb_size filtering false \"UBI#\" occurrences inside PEB *data* (not at PEB boundaries). E.g., UBIFS data containing \"UBI#\" bytes → false offset → interval not multiple of peb → gcd collapses. With magic-only filtering, a single false occurrence breaks the gcd. With CRC filtering, false occurrences are dropped. That's valuable. But the corrupt-dump regression: all-CRC-broken image → InvalidInputFormat → no chunk (old code worked). \n\nMiddle ground: filter candidate offsets by CRC *if enough valid headers exist*; else fall back to magic+version only:\n\n```python\nvalid = [o for o in candidates if o >= start and self._parse_ec_header(...) is not None]\nif len(valid) >= 2: use valid\nelse: use magic+version-filtered offsets (o >= start)\n```\n\nHmm, adds complexity. Is it justified? The corrupt-EC-everywhere dump is pathological; but \"never silently fail to extract what we used to extract\" matters. The fallback is ~4 lines. I'll do: try CRC-validated offsets first; if fewer than 2, fall back to version-checked offsets (magic+version, no CRC). Actually wait — for the walk, similar concern: if I require CRC validity to count a PEB as good, an all-corrupt-CRC image walks only PEB0 (first_peb special-cased) then 20 bads → chunk = 1 PEB. Old code would walk the whole image. Regression! So the walk should also adapt: use CRC-validity if the image's headers are generally CRC-valid, else magic+version.\n\nSimpler unified approach: decide per-image \"strictness\" in calculate_chunk: \n\n```python\nimage_offsets_valid = crc-valid offsets >= start\nif len(valid) >= 2: strict (use CRC+seq) else: lenient (magic+version)\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me reconsider — how likely is a firmware dump with systematically broken EC CRCs? EC header CRC is written by ubinize/UBI itself; dumps preserve it. Broken-CRC-everywhere basically doesn't happen in practice (unlike JFFS2 where CRC quirks are common). ubireader computes and reports it. Real bad blocks → garbage. I'll go strict (CRC required for non-first PEBs), no fallback. It gives clean semantics:\n- valid EC header (magic+version+CRC) + matching image_seq → same image, continue\n- valid EC header + different image_seq → another image → stop\n- invalid → bad block → skip up to 20\n\nAnd document in limitations: \"PEBs are validated with their EC header CRC; images with systematically corrupted eraseblock headers are not fully recognized.\" Hmm wait, actually, let me reconsider once more... The user's scenario is *automotive firmware with bad blocks* — standard ubinize images + bad blocks. Strict CRC works perfectly there. I'll go strict. And the first PEB is exempt (pattern-matched; also handles the case where PEB0's CRC is broken but rest are fine).\n\nWait, one more edge in _guess_peb_size with seq: I filter offsets by (valid CRC) and (seq == seq of first valid offset ≥ start_offset). But what if start_offset's PEB has broken CRC and the *next* valid header belongs to a *different* concatenated image? Pathological; ignore.\n\nNow — hmm, actually one more important case: what if the same blob contains the same UBI image's PEBs but the blob also has OTHER \"UBI#\" data far away (e.g. another partition with a UBI image of same seq? unlikely). Fine.\n\nLet me also double check `get_endian` usage stays for UBIFS. Yes.\n\nNow write everything. Also check `Handler` import still needed (UBIHandler becomes StructHandler; UBIFSHandler already StructHandler). `iterate_patterns` still used. `statistics` import dropped, add `math`, `zlib`. `SeekError` still used in walk. `get_intervals` still used.\n\nLet me now check models.StructHandler more closely and Endian import location."}],"encrypted_content":null,"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc"}}}
