rt_offset, which would be the first PEB itself. OK, consistent.\n\nAnother subtlety: `_walk_ubi` end when `ec_header is None` due to EOF (read returns short) — counted as bad, then seek beyond EOF raises SeekError → break. But that burns one \"bad\" count; fine. However, there's a subtle inefficiency: after EOF, `file.seek(offset + peb_size)` — seeking beyond EOF in this File class raises SeekError? Original code caught SeekError on seek beyond EOF, so yes.\n\nBut wait: what if seek beyond EOF does NOT raise (some file objects allow it)? Then loop continues: tell() = beyond EOF, read returns b\"\" → None → bad++ ... until 20 bads → break. Bounded. Fine.\n\nAlso `ValidChunk` import still used; `Handler` import removed — check no other use of `Handler` in the file. UBIExtractor extends Command. OK.\n\nOne more: `_guess_peb_size` — `ec_headers.append((offset, ec_header.image_seq))`. Then intervals filtered by seq. Good.\n\nPotential issue: `iterate_patterns(file, ...)` — file position: it restores initial position when exhausted. In calculate_chunk, file is at start_offset when called (unblob seeks there). list() exhausts → position restored to start_offset. Then _parse_ec_header seeks around. Then `file.seek(start_offset)` in calculate_chunk. Good.\n\nNow — UBIFS validation: `sb_header.ch.len` — cstruct field `len`... is `len` a problem like `size`? AGENTS.md says don't name a field `size` (shadows cstruct's .size). `len` shadows nothing in cstruct? The existing C_DEFINITIONS already had `uint32 len` in ubifs_ch and it parsed fine before. OK.\n\n`default_compr` is uint16 in the struct. UBIFSCompression(sb_header.default_compr) works.\n\nNow quick syntax/import check and run existing tests."]},"started_at_ms":1794660418436,"completed_at_ms":1794660436182}}
