{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:48:33.073Z","ordinal":354,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_e9b6bff81df7a996e11e1795","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["Kernel v6.12: UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION = 5. But banana fixture has fmt_version=4. So format version 4 exists in the wild (older mkfs.ubifs). Version history: UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION was 4 for a long time; bumped to 5 recently (for authenticated... or for the \"hash\" entries?). So gate should accept a range: 1 <= fmt_version <= 5? Or just `fmt_version in (4, 5)`? Hmm. ubireader doesn't check fmt_version at all. To be permissive but still gate garbage: accept `1 <= fmt_version <= 5`? Random garbage uint32 would almost always fail that. But future version 6 images would be rejected... AGENTS.md: \"Keep file values as plain int where vendors may add values\" — fmt_version could grow. Better: gate `0 < fmt_version <= 5`? If kernel bumps to 6, handler rejects valid images until updated — that's the trade-off of format gates. Common practice in unblob handlers: check exact known versions. Let me check what other handlers do... e.g. they often check `version != EXPECTED`. I'll accept 4 and 5 (the versions that exist), since UBIFS on-flash format has only ever been 4 and 5 in practice (versions 1-3 were pre-mainline experimental). Actually let me double-check: was it ever < 4 in mainline? Original mainline UBIFS (2008, 2.6.27) had format version... I believe mainline started at 4? Hmm, not sure. To be safe: `fmt_version > 5 or fmt_version == 0 → reject`? Hmm, that accepts 1-3 which may never have existed... Accepting them is harmless (ubireader will just try). I'll gate: `fmt_version == 0 or fmt_version > 5` → invalid. Wait, but that's a weird gate. Alternatively just `fmt_version > UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION (5)`. Zero is clearly garbage. Let me do:\n\n```python\nif not 1 <= sb_header.fmt_version <= cls._UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION:  # 5\n```\n\nAlso check `ro_compat_version`: UBIFS_RO_COMPAT_VERSION = 0. Gate `ro_compat_version != 0`? Hmm — ro_compat_version is designed so that if it's nonzero and unknown, kernel mounts ro... Actually UBIFS_RO_COMPAT_VERSION is the highest ro-compat version the kernel supports (=0). A valid image could have ro_compat_version up to... it's a feature bitmask-ish version. If image has ro_compat_version > 0, current kernels can't fully understand. Gate `> 0`? Real images have 0. I'll skip gating ro_compat (keep it minimal) — no, actually it's a decent gate... skip. Keep gates minimal but effective.\n\nFix the constant: `_UBIFS_FORMAT_VERSION = 5` (max supported), check `1 <= fmt_version <= 5`.\n\nAlso update the doc reference — fine as is.\n\nNow also check orange fixture's UBIFS volumes pass the gates (rootfs.ubifs from orange image — but that one is truncated and not extracted; configuration.ubifs is extracted). The ubi integration test passed already, which includes configuration.ubifs extraction → its superblock passed gates. Good.\n\nFix and rerun."]},"started_at_ms":1794660492787,"completed_at_ms":1794660513073}}
