{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:49:52.330Z","ordinal":396,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_33d494e8ed86114003cffc8a","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["peb_size guess works: 131072 (128 KiB). So the walk must be ending early or the chunk... wait, chunk distribution showed no UBI chunk at all. Maybe the walk returned end covering the whole image (past EOF?) — orange is truncated: file size 3430400 = 26.17 * 131072. The last PEB is partial. Walk: PEBs at 0, 131072, ..., 25*131072=3276800; PEB 25 valid? 26*131072 = 3407872; file is 3430400, so PEB 26 starts at 3407872, EC header at 3407872 (64 bytes available: 3430400-3407872=22528 ≥ 64). If PEB 26's EC header is valid, end_offset = 3407872+131072 = 3538944 > file size → chunk dropped by unblob (runs past end)! \n\nOld code: walked while magic matches; PEB 26 has \"UBI#\" magic → end = 3407872... wait old code returned `offset` (start of last failed PEB), so old end = 3407872 even if PEB26 had valid magic? No — old code: reads magic at each PEB; if matches, seek to next; returns offset of first non-matching PEB. If PEB26 matched, seek to 3538944 → SeekError → break → return offset=3407872. So old chunk = 0..3407872 (matches expected output `0-3407872.ubi`).\n\nNew code: PEB26 valid → end_offset = 3538944 → past EOF → chunk dropped → UBI unknown. That's the regression!\n\nFix: when the next PEB would extend past EOF (SeekError on seek, or short read), the chunk should end at the last *complete* PEB. In my walk, after processing PEB26 (valid), I set end_offset = 3538944, then seek fails → break → return 3538944. Wrong.\n\nHow to handle: end_offset should be min(end_offset, ...)? AGENTS.md says don't clip to file size... but that's about taking sizes from structures rather than file size. Here the issue: the image is truncated; the last PEB is incomplete. The valid chunk should cover only complete PEBs. Hmm — but \"unblob drops chunks that run past the end, which leaves truncated input unknown\" — the guidance is to not clip *sizes from structures* to the file. But here, a truncated final PEB genuinely isn't part of the carvable image... The old behavior carved up to the last PEB boundary within the file.\n\nOptions:\n1. When seek to offset+peb_size raises SeekError (i.e., next PEB start would be past EOF), break with end_offset = offset (start of current PEB) if the current PEB is incomplete... but we can't know if current PEB is complete without checking file size. SeekError tells us offset+peb_size > file size → current PEB is incomplete → don't include it: end_offset stays at previous value (which is `offset` of current PEB = previous end). \n\nSo: set end_offset = offset + peb_size only if that position is seekable? Restructure:\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    offset = file.tell()\n    ec_header = self._parse_ec_header(file, offset)\n    ...\n    try:\n        file.seek(offset + peb_size)\n    except SeekError:\n        # the next PEB would start past the end of the file: the current\n        # PEB is truncated, don't include it in the chunk\n        break\n    if valid: end_offset = offset + peb_size\n```\n\nWait, order: seek first, then update end_offset. If seek succeeds, PEB [offset, offset+peb_size) is fully within the file → include it. If seek fails → PEB truncated → exclude → break. That gives old behavior for orange: PEB26 valid but 3538944 unseekable → break → end = 3407872. \n\nBut careful: is seek to exactly EOF allowed? If offset+peb_size == file_size, seek should succeed (position at EOF is valid). Then end_offset = file_size. Next iteration: read returns b\"\" → None → bad++ → seek(offset+peb_size) beyond EOF → SeekError → break. Good.\n\nEdge: does File.seek raise SeekError beyond EOF? Old code relied on it, yes.\n\nRestructure walk:\n\n```python\n        while True:\n            offset = file.tell()\n            ec_header = self._parse_ec_header(file, offset)\n            if ec_header is not None and ec_header.image_seq != image_seq:\n                break\n            try:\n                file.seek(offset + peb_size)\n            except SeekError:\n                # The current PEB is truncated, the image ends before it\n                break\n            if first_peb or ec_header is not None:\n                consecutive_bad_pebs = 0\n                end_offset = offset + peb_size\n            else:\n                consecutive_bad_pebs += 1\n                if consecutive_bad_pebs > self._MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BAD_PEBS:\n                    break\n            first_peb = False\n        return end_offset\n```\n\nHmm wait, but now a truncated final PEB that is a *bad block* — same handling, fine.\n\nBut there's a subtlety with the bad-block limit check: after incrementing bad count and not exceeding limit, we continue; each iteration seeks further. When we finally exceed, break. end_offset = last valid PEB end. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: `first_peb` with truncated first PEB (image of exactly one partial PEB): offset=0, parse maybe valid (64 bytes present), seek(0+peb) fails → break → end_offset = initial file.tell() = start_offset → chunk 0..0 → invalid. unblob rejects end<=start? ValidChunk with start==end — probably raises or gets dropped. calculate_chunk returning empty chunk — AGENTS.md says unblob rejects chunks that end at or before start; we shouldn't re-check. OK.\n\nAlso `_parse_ec_header` reads 64 bytes — for a truncated PEB with <64 bytes remaining, returns None → treated as bad → but then seek fails → break. Fine.\n\nLet me apply this fix."]},"started_at_ms":1794660562570,"completed_at_ms":1794660592330}}
