{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:55:09.564Z","ordinal":515,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_80c35a0bc68328019eaabb34","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["ubireader: for each PEB-sized block, if it starts with \"UBI#\", it builds a description and accesses `blk.vid_hdr.data_size` — vid_hdr is None when the VID magic isn't found at vid_hdr_offset (our bad PEB has 0xEE there) → crash. So ubireader crashes on PEBs that have EC magic but no VID magic. If the bad PEB does NOT start with \"UBI#\" at all, it goes to the `else` branch (probably just skipped). \n\nSo for the fixture, make bad PEBs NOT start with \"UBI#\" — e.g. filled with 0x00 or garbage without the magic. That's realistic: bad blocks often read as all 0xFF/0x00 or random garbage. Let me check the else branch quickly... presumably logs \"no EC header\" and continues. Let me change bad_peb() to 0xEE fill entirely (no magic). But then my unit test's bad_peb (with magic + bad CRC) tests handler robustness — keep that in unit tests (our handler tolerates it), but the integration fixture must be ubireader-compatible: use fully garbage PEBs.\n\nWait — but also consider: real bad blocks in dumps — ubireader would crash on those too if they contain \"UBI#\" + garbage VID. That's ubireader's limitation, not ours. Our handler carves correctly; extraction failure gets reported as ExtractionProblem. For the integration fixture, use magic-less garbage so the full flow succeeds.\n\nUpdate generator: bad_peb() = b\"\\xee\" * PEB_SIZE? Or more realistic: 0xFF fill (erased block) — but an erased block isn't \"bad\"… A bad block typically has garbage. I'll use a deterministic pseudo-garbage pattern. Let me use bytes(range(256)) repeated, or simply 0xEE. I'll use 0xEE fill — clearly not UBI."]},"started_at_ms":1794660899529,"completed_at_ms":1794660909564}}
