{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T12:57:43.180Z","ordinal":609,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_96ecf8de1dae92698eaf5004","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["Two things:\n1. vulture on single file gives false positives (C_DEFINITIONS etc. are used by the framework) — vulture runs on the whole codebase with whitelist in pre-commit. Run vulture the way pre-commit does (whole project). Let me check the pre-commit vulture args.\n\n2. test_encryption_metadata rar failure — probably unrelated to my change (rar handler). Verify it fails on a clean tree? Let me check git stash... quicker: this test doesn't touch ubi. Check if it fails due to missing LFS files (tests/files or integration inputs for rar are LFS and I only fetched ubi ones!). Right — other integration fixtures are still LFS pointers. That's environmental, not my change. The full suite needs `git lfs pull` which I can't do (no git-lfs). I could fetch all LFS files via the media endpoint like before... that's a lot of files but doable. Actually, let me just fetch all LFS files for the whole repo — it makes the full suite runnable. How many files? git ls-files | grep tests/integration + tests/files... Let me count. It took ~1.5s per file earlier; could be hundreds. Let me count first.\n\nActually, for validating my change, running the full suite isn't strictly necessary — but it's good to verify nothing else broke. My change only touches ubi handler + new tests. Relevant suites: tests/handlers/, tests/test_handlers.py. The rar failure is due to LFS pointers (environmental). Let me confirm that specific failure is LFS-related, then decide whether to bulk-fetch LFS."]},"started_at_ms":1794661058239,"completed_at_ms":1794661063179}}
