{"timestamp":"2026-11-14T13:10:18.122Z","ordinal":772,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d9fc-8ce9-7420-953c-6c7a3fab8a59","turn_id":"01a1d9fc-8cfb-7500-aadc-dd421289bedc","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_7ca9092d214ec4325b56d6c6","summary_text":[],"raw_content":["The stash/confirm worked: on the pristine tree, `tests/test_report.py tests/test_cleanup.py` had \"5 failed, 4 passed\" — same failures without my change. So those 81 failures are pre-existing/environmental (missing external extractors like 7z). Stash popped, my changes are back.\n\nWait — the stash output shows \"5 failed\" for test_report+test_cleanup on clean tree. With my change earlier full run: test_cleanup 3 + test_report 2 = 5. Consistent. \n\nNote: `git stash -u` stashed my new fixture files too and pop restored them. Verify ubi tests still pass after the stash cycle, and confirm git status shows my files. Also the docs/demo.gif got modified by LFS fetch (pointer → real content) — that's fine, it's the actual LFS content now.\n\nOne more thing: `artifacts/` is untracked — was that there before? It was in the original `ls` output at the very beginning (artifacts dir existed). Untracked but pre-existing. Leave it.\n\nNow let me do a final verification run of the ubi-related tests and confirm the failing set is identical with/without my change. I already know: 81 failures both environmental. Let me just double-check that the ubi tests all pass and quickly re-verify the complete failing list contains no ubi/filesystem tests... Earlier grep showed failed test files: test_handlers.py (57), test_processing.py (13), test_processing_suffixes.py (6), test_cleanup.py (3), test_report.py (2) = 81. The 57 test_handlers failures — are any of them filesystem.ubi? Earlier when I grepped FAILED lines excluding \"ubi\", count was still 81 — wait, that grep -iv \"ubi\" filtered the FAILED lines before counting... The awk/uniq output summed to 81 and the total grep -c FAILED was also 81 — but the first command excluded ubi lines. Hmm: `grep FAILED | grep -iv \"ubi\"` then counted by file → 81 total from non-ubi lines. And total FAILED = 81. So zero ubi failures. \n\nBut wait — could \"ubi\" appear in other test names (e.g. \"public\")? grep -iv \"ubi\" would exclude lines containing \"ubi\" anywhere... \"dlink\" no... Hmm, paths like tests/integration/... don't contain \"ubi\" except ubi tests. OK good.\n\nLet me run the final check: ubi tests + a re-run of the previously failing set on the current tree to confirm same 81. Actually I already have that. Just re-run ubi tests to make sure stash pop restored everything correctly.\n\nAlso, I should double check `git diff` on docs/demo.gif and tests/files — those are LFS content replacements (pointer → real data), showing as \"modified\". That's expected and desirable (equivalent to git lfs pull). But it makes the working tree dirty with 2900 modified files. When the user commits, they should commit only my change-related files. Hmm — actually with git-lfs not installed, `git add` of those files would commit real content as regular files, bypassing LFS. I should warn the user about this in the final message: the LFS files were fetched via direct download (git-lfs not installed); before committing, install git-lfs and run `git lfs pull` / checkout, or commit carefully.\n\nActually wait — will git see the downloaded files as \"modified\"? Yes because without the LFS smudge filter, git compares the working file (real content) against the index (pointer). With git-lfs installed, `git status` would show them clean (filter converts). So my final message should note: install git-lfs to normalize the view; the fetched content is authentic (verified oid? I verified one earlier — the sha256 matched the pointer for apple.ubifs).\n\nLet me verify a couple of the re-fetched files' integrity against their pointer oids... the pointers are gone now (replaced). I could check git: `git show HEAD:path` gives pointer with oid; compare sha256 of working file. Let me spot-check a few, especially the 6 special-character ones and my ubi fixtures. Actually for ubi fixtures I verified content earlier (tests pass). Spot-check 3 random ones.\n\nThen final: run the ubi test suite once more + ruff/pyright full-file checks, write final summary. Also update the plan? I never created a plan via update_plan. It's fine — work is nearly done.\n\nAlso should I update README's format list? README line 22 already mentions UBI/UBIFS. The \"格式列表\" is docs/handlers.md — regenerated. mkdocs.yml — check if there's a formats page referencing handlers.md. It's generated from handlers. Done.\n\nOne more consideration — the user asked \"识别 UBI 与 UBIFS 的超级块和卷\" — done via patterns + validation. \"按卷解出 UBIFS 并提取文件\" — verified end-to-end with the new fixture (banana volume → UBIFS → files; config volume → raw). \"处理坏块与多卷\" — done. \"补测试镜像\" — unit tests + integration fixture. \"更新文档与格式列表\" — handlers.md regenerated.\n\nSpot-check LFS integrity, then final ubi test run."]},"started_at_ms":1794661775261,"completed_at_ms":1794661818122}}
